{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## CITATION-3B5F-D: the inherited b9b8 claim is HALF right — and so is the review that refuted it\n\n`review-plan` blocked on this, correctly flagging that I had carried an inherited citation without\nchecking it. Checked now. Both accounts are partly wrong.\n\n**The ticket (and the handoff) said:** \"Ticket b9b8 already reworked the live DC cell\n`test_a_deleted_dc_issue_is_planned_as_an_inbound_probe` for exactly this reason.\"\n\n**The reviewer said:** refuted — \"b9b8's actual scope is title-mirroring (`outbound_field_diff`)\".\n\n**The truth:** `b9b8-1c40-ea53-4ef3` is titled *\"inbound title reverted by outbound local-wins\"* and\nits root cause is `outbound_field_diff.py:38-45` omitting `title` from `_INBOUND_MIRRORED_FIELDS`. So\nthe reviewer is right about the ticket's SCOPE. But the rework really did happen, and it really did\nride b9b8's commit:\n\n    commit ae009443a9  \"b9b8: mirror a Jira-side title edit inbound\"\n      tests/external/live_jira_dc/test_dc_mutations.py\n      - def test_a_deleted_dc_issue_is_planned_as_an_inbound_probe(\n      + def test_a_deleted_dc_issue_never_plans_a_local_teardown(\n\nSo the cell WAS renamed and re-oracled, in a commit whose ticket was about something else entirely.\nThe claim is substantively correct about what happened and wrong about why — it was **bundled into\nb9b8's commit, not part of b9b8's scope.** The reviewer's \"refuted\" overshoots; the ticket's \"for\nexactly this reason\" overclaims.\n\n**Third instance of this pattern in this epic.** 2bc7's fix landed under J4's commit\n(`rebar-ticket: 0a94`), which is why 2bc7's close could not find a referencing commit and got forced.\nThis cell's rework landed under b9b8's. Both times the consequence was the same: the work is real, but\nit is not attributable from the ticket, so a later reader either cannot find it or cites the wrong\ncause. Worth naming as a recurring cost of bundling, not just as two anecdotes.\n\nThe description is corrected to state the bundling precisely. The substantive point the citation was\nsupporting — that the cell no longer asserts an `(inbound, probe)` observable, because no production\npath emits one — is unaffected and independently verified: the current cell asserts the ADR 0028 §1\nteardown observable and PASSED live in harness run 30933704255."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785871578829162001,"uuid":"143422b9-9c32-41bb-bf8a-9140ba5f564b"}